Commentary of al-Shinqiti (Adwa' al-Bayan) on surah 8, verse 29:

His saying, exalted is He: ﴿O you who have believed, if you fear Allah, He will grant you a criterion (furqan) and will remove from you your misdeeds and forgive you. And Allah is the possessor of great bounty.﴾

Ibn 'Abbas, al-Suddi, Mujahid, 'Ikrimah, al-Dahhak, Qatadah, Muqatil ibn Hayyan, and others besides them said: "Furqan" means a way out (makhraj). Mujahid added: "in this world and the Hereafter." In another narration from Ibn 'Abbas: "Furqan" means deliverance (najah), and in another narration from him: victory (nasr). Muhammad ibn Ishaq said: "Furqan" means a distinction (fasl) between truth and falsehood. This is reported by Ibn Kathir.

The one who writes this, may Allah pardon him, says: The view of the group mentioned, that the intended meaning of furqan is "a way out," is supported by His saying, exalted is He: ﴿And whoever fears Allah, He will make for him a way out﴾ [al-Talaq: 2]. The view that it means deliverance or victory returns in meaning to this, since whoever Allah makes a way out for, He delivers and gives victory. However, the view that the Qur'an and the Arabic language indicate as correct for interpreting the aforementioned verse is the saying of Ibn Ishaq. This is because furqan (الفُرْقان) is a verbal noun (masdar) to which the alif and nun have been added, and it is intended as a descriptive term, i.e., that which distinguishes between truth and falsehood. This is its meaning in His saying: ﴿Blessed is He who sent down the Criterion (al-furqan)﴾ [al-Furqan: 1], i.e., the Book that distinguishes between truth and falsehood;

• and His saying: ﴿And He sent down the Criterion﴾ [Al 'Imran: 4];

• and His saying: ﴿And when We gave Moses the Book and the Criterion﴾ [al-Baqarah: 53];

• and His saying: ﴿And We gave Moses and Aaron the Criterion﴾ [al-Anbiya': 48]. What indicates that the intended meaning of furqan here is the knowledge that distinguishes between truth and falsehood is His saying, exalted is He, in surah al-Hadid: ﴿O you who have believed, fear Allah and believe in His Messenger; He will give you a double portion of His mercy, and will make for you a light by which you walk, and will forgive you﴾ up to the end of the verse [al-Hadid: 28].

Because His saying here, ﴿and will make for you a light by which you walk﴾, means knowledge and guidance by which you distinguish between truth and falsehood. What indicates that the intended meaning of light here is guidance and recognition of the truth is His saying, concerning one who was a disbeliever and whom Allah guided: ﴿Are those who were dead and whom We brought to life and gave a light by which they walk among the people...﴾ up to the end of the verse [al-An'am: 122]. So He made the light mentioned in al-Hadid the same as the furqan mentioned in al-Anfal, as you see. The removal of misdeeds and the forgiveness that are arranged upon fear of Allah in the verse of al-Anfal are likewise arranged upon it in the verse of al-Hadid, and this is a clear explanation, as you see.
